nfs3.xfs -- an NFS version 3 client file system for FreeMiNT
============================================================

This is a client implementation of NFS version 3 (RFC 1813) for FreeMiNT.
It is derived from the NFS version 2 driver (sys/xfs/nfs, RFC 1094),
which stays in the tree unchanged: the two drivers can be
installed at the same time and mount their exports independently.

The reason this driver exists is simple: current Linux distributions
(Ubuntu 24.04 among them) no longer ship NFSv2 support in their kernel
nfsd, so nfs.xfs cannot mount anything from them any more. NFSv3 is still
supported everywhere.


INSTALLATION
============

  - Make sure the MiNT socket device (inet4) is working and that
    u:\etc\hosts, u:\etc\services and u:\etc\resolv.conf are in place.
  - Copy nfs3.xfs into your \mint\<version>\ directory so that MiNT loads
    it at boot time. It installs itself as u:\nfs3.
  - Copy mount_nfs3 (from tools/nfs3) somewhere in your PATH.

On the server side, export the directory as usual, for example in
/etc/exports on a Linux box:

    /srv/atari  192.168.1.0/24(rw,sync,no_subtree_check,insecure)

`insecure' is only needed if the Atari cannot bind a reserved port.
No special nfsvers= option is required -- Linux offers NFSv3 by default.

Mounting:

    mount_nfs3 server:/srv/atari /nfs3/atari
    mount_nfs3 -o ro,rsize=8192 server:/srv/pub /nfs3/pub

Unmounting:

    mount_nfs3 -u /nfs3/atari

The mount point below /nfs3 is created by the driver, it does not have
to exist beforehand.


OPTIONS
=======

  tcp, udp          pin the transport, also spelled proto=tcp and
                    proto=udp. Without either, TCP is tried first and
                    UDP is used if that fails; see TRANSPORT below
  ro, rw            mount read only / read write
  nosuid, suid      ignore / honour the set-uid bit
  rsize=n, wsize=n  transfer sizes; clamped to what FSINFO3 reports
  timeo=n           initial timeout in tenths of a second
  retrans=n         number of retries before giving up
  port=n            NFS port on the server (default 2049)
  actimeo=n         attribute cache lifetime in tenths of a second


TRANSPORT: UDP OR TCP
=====================

TCP is the default: the driver connects, and only if that fails does it
fall back to UDP for that mount. Two reasons TCP comes first:

  - A current Linux nfsd serves TCP out of the box, while UDP has to be
    switched on with udp=y in /etc/nfs.conf. Over TCP the driver works
    against an unmodified server.
  - A reply larger than the MTU travels as several IP fragments over UDP,
    and losing any one of them loses the whole reply. On a busy WLAN that
    happens often enough to matter. TCP retransmits the missing segment
    instead, and does its own congestion control.

The implementation adds RPC record marking (RFC 5531, section 11) and
reassembly of records that arrive in pieces, keeps one connection per
server and rebuilds it when the stream breaks. Retransmission is left to
TCP: a request is sent once and only sent again after a reconnect.

"-o tcp" and "-o udp" pin the transport, so a mount that must not
silently change protocol can say so. Which one a mount ended up using is
printed when it succeeds:

    nfs3: mounted 'atari' from fileserver over TCP

That line matters because an "-o tcp" against a driver too old to know
the option looks exactly like a working TCP mount from the outside.


LIMITATIONS
===========

  - Files larger than 2 GB can be looked at (Fstat64 reports the true
    size) but not read or written past the 2 GB mark, because MiNT's
    file position is a signed 32 bit value.
  - ACCESS3 is implemented in the XDR layer but not wired into the file
    system yet; permission checks still rely on the mode bits, as they
    did under NFS2.
  - READDIRPLUS3 and MKNOD3 are not implemented. READDIRPLUS would need
    considerably more buffer space than a small machine can spare, and
    MiNT's XFS interface only offers mknod through FS_EXT_1, which this
    driver does not implement (neither does the NFS2 one).
  - Only AUTH_UNIX is supported, as in the NFS2 driver. There is no
    Kerberos (RPCSEC_GSS).
  - The UDP fallback is decided once, at mount time. A server that
    stops answering over TCP later does not switch protocol.


FILES
=====

  nfs3_xdr.c/h   the RFC 1813 data types
  xdr.c/h        generic XDR, extended with 64 bit and variable opaque
  rpc_xdr.c/h    ONC RPC message headers (unchanged from the NFS2 driver)
  sock_ipc.c/h   UDP and TCP transport, retransmission
  nfssys.c       the FILESYS entry points
  nfsdev.c       the DEVDRV entry points (read, write, ioctl, ...)
  nfsutil.c      fattr3 <-> XATTR conversion, error mapping
  index.c        file index management (unchanged from the NFS2 driver)
  cache.c        lookup cache (unchanged from the NFS2 driver)
  main.c         installation


WHAT IS DIFFERENT IN NFS VERSION 3
==================================

The following is the list of protocol changes that actually mattered for
this implementation. Section numbers refer to RFC 1813.

File handles (2.5, 3.3)
-----------------------
NFS2 file handles are a fixed 32 byte blob. NFS3 handles are variable
sized, up to 64 bytes ("opaque data<NFS3_FHSIZE>"), and Linux really uses
that: the handle length depends on the exported file system and on the
export options. Every place that stores or transmits a handle therefore
has to carry the length as well; see nfs_fh3 in nfs3_xdr.h.

64 bit file sizes and offsets (2.5)
-----------------------------------
size3, offset3, fileid3 and cookie3 are 64 bit. This is the change that
lifts the 2/4 GB file size limit of NFS2. MiNT's XATTR, FILEPTR.pos and
the GEMDOS interface are 32 bit, so the driver keeps the full 64 bit size
in NFS_INDEX.size (used by Fstat64() and by lseek(SEEK_END)) and clamps
to 0x7fffffff wherever it has to hand a value to a 32 bit interface.

File type and mode (2.5)
------------------------
In NFS2 the file type was encoded into the high bits of fattr.mode, using
the traditional Unix S_IF* values -- which are *not* MiNT's S_IF* values,
so the old driver needed its own N_IF* constants and a translation table.
NFS3 has a separate ftype3 field and mode3 carries the protection bits
only. ftype3 maps one to one onto MiNT's file types, including sockets
and fifos, which NFS2 could not express properly at all.

Also new in fattr3: `used' (bytes actually allocated, 64 bit) replaces
`blocks' (512 byte units), `blocksize' is gone, `rdev' became a
structured specdata3 and `fsid' became 64 bit.

Setting attributes (3.3.2)
--------------------------
NFS2's sattr used the convention "a field of all ones means do not
change this". NFS3's sattr3 is a discriminated union per attribute, and
for the two time stamps it can additionally say "set it to the server's
current time" (SET_TO_SERVER_TIME), which the driver uses for touch(1)
style operations so that a wrong Atari clock does not matter.
SETATTR3 also gained an optional guard (sattrguard3) that makes the
operation conditional on the object's ctime; the driver does not use it.

Optional attributes in every reply (2.6)
----------------------------------------
Nearly every NFS3 reply carries a post_op_attr (the object's attributes
after the operation) or a wcc_data (attributes before and after, for
"weak cache consistency"), and it carries them in the failure case too.
They have to be decoded whether they are wanted or not. The driver feeds
them into its attribute cache, so a lookup, a read or a write refreshes
the cached attributes for free -- something NFS2 needed extra GETATTR
round trips for.

Procedure numbering and new procedures (3.3)
--------------------------------------------
LOOKUP moved from procedure 4 to 3, the obsolete NFS2 procedures ROOT (3)
and WRITECACHE (7) are gone, and everything behind LOOKUP shifted. New
procedures are ACCESS (4), MKNOD (11), READDIRPLUS (17), FSINFO (19),
PATHCONF (20) and COMMIT (21); STATFS (17) was replaced by FSSTAT (18).

FSINFO3 is used at mount time to find out the transfer sizes the server
prefers and whether it supports hard links and symbolic links at all;
NFS2 clients had to guess, and the old driver simply hard wired 4 kB.

FSSTAT3 reports free space in bytes as 64 bit numbers instead of NFS2's
block counts. nfs_dfree() converts that into the 32 bit
clusters/sectors that GEMDOS expects, using 1 kB units so that even
multi terabyte exports do not overflow.

Object creation (3.3.8 - 3.3.11)
--------------------------------
CREATE3 has three modes (UNCHECKED, GUARDED, EXCLUSIVE); the driver uses
UNCHECKED, which is what NFS2's CREATE did, because MiNT's kernel already
handles O_EXCL. More importantly, CREATE3/MKDIR3/SYMLINK3/MKNOD3 return
the new file handle only *optionally* (post_op_fh3). When the server
leaves it out, the driver marks the new index as handle-less and does a
LOOKUP3 the first time the handle is actually needed -- the same
mechanism that was already there for entries coming out of READDIR.

Note also that SYMLINK3 sends the attributes *before* the target path,
where NFS2 sent the path first.

The driver no longer asks for a specific uid/gid when creating a file.
NFS2's driver did, which fails on servers that squash the client's
credentials; NFS3 servers assign owner and group from the RPC credentials
anyway.

READDIR (3.3.16)
----------------
Directory cookies are 64 bit, and the reply carries an 8 byte cookie
verifier that has to be sent back unchanged with every follow-up request
so that the server can detect that the directory was rewritten in
between. The driver keeps both in the DIR handle's fsstuff area.

READDIRPLUS (17), which would deliver attributes and file handles along
with the names and would save the follow-up LOOKUP3 per entry, is not
implemented: it costs a lot of buffer space on a machine with a few
megabytes of RAM, and many servers turn it off anyway.

WRITE and COMMIT (3.3.7, 3.3.21)
--------------------------------
This is the big performance change. NFS2's WRITE was always synchronous:
the server had to have the data on disk before it could answer. NFS3
lets the client write UNSTABLE and flush later with COMMIT3.

nfs_write() writes UNSTABLE and issues a single COMMIT3 for the whole
range before it returns, so a write() call is as durable as it was under
NFS2 but needs only one synchronous round trip instead of one per
wsize-sized chunk. If the write verifier that COMMIT3 returns differs
from the one the WRITE3 replies carried, the server rebooted and lost the
data; the driver then rewrites the range with FILE_SYNC.

Error codes (2.6)
-----------------
NFS3 added NFS3ERR_INVAL, NFS3ERR_XDEV, NFS3ERR_MLINK, NFS3ERR_NOTSUPP,
NFS3ERR_BAD_COOKIE, NFS3ERR_JUKEBOX, NFS3ERR_SERVERFAULT and others, and
dropped NFSERR_WFLUSH. The NFS2 driver mostly answered EACCES no matter
what went wrong; this one translates the status properly (nfs3_error()
in nfsutil.c), so applications see ENOSPC, EDQUOT, ENOTEMPTY, ESTALE and
friends.

MOUNT protocol
--------------
Version 3 of the MOUNT protocol (RFC 1813 appendix I) is used, because
version 1 can only return a 32 byte handle. Its reply also lists the
authentication flavours the server accepts. This lives in the
mount_nfs3 utility, see tools/nfs3.

What did *not* change
---------------------
The ONC RPC layer (RFC 1057) and the AUTH_UNIX credentials are the same
as in NFS2, so rpc_xdr.c was taken over from the NFS2 driver unchanged
and sock_ipc.c kept its UDP path. The general shape of the
driver -- index clusters, lookup cache, mount slots -- was kept from the
NFS version 2 driver, so that fixes can be moved between the two.
